    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Oyo Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yoruba">Yoruba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#savannah">savannah</a> state whose cavalry made it the leading power between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-river">Niger</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volta-river">Volta</a>.</li><li>It grew rich on tribute and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassalage</a> and shaping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a> coast.</li><li>Feuds between king and chiefs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afonja">Afonja</a>'s revolt and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilorin">Ilorin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emirate">emirate</a> destroyed it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oyo-ile">Oyo-Ile</a> was abandoned c. 1835–1836.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Oyo's council of chiefs could reject an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alaafin">Alaafin</a> by presenting him with an empty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#calabash">calabash</a> or parrots' eggs, after which the king was expected to take his own life.</p></aside>
    </section>

