    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#oranmiyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oranmiyan.jpg" alt="Oranmiyan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Opa_Oranmiyan_at_Ile-Ife_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Abby's Lens, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Oyo Empire originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#savannah">savannah</a> woodlands of northern Yorubaland, north of the dense rainforest zone that constrained cavalry-based state formation across much of coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-africa">West Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yoruba">Yoruba</a> oral tradition attributes its founding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a>, remembered as a son or grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oduduwa">Oduduwa</a>, the mythical progenitor of the Yoruba people, who established the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oyo-ile">Oyo-Ile</a>, also called Old Oyo or Katunga, as the dynasty’s sacred capital, probably in the fourteenth century. The location of Oyo-Ile on open savannah enabled the horse-based military culture that would become the empire’s principal instrument of expansion, distinguishing Oyo from the forest Yoruba kingdoms to its south, where cavalry was of little use. The early centuries were precarious: Oyo faced persistent pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> kingdom to the north-east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bariba">Bariba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#borgu">Borgu</a> to the north-west, and in the early sixteenth century a crushing defeat by the Nupe forced the ruling dynasty into roughly eighty years of exile, much of it at Igboho. This period of dispossession paradoxically strengthened Oyo’s military culture through sustained contact with the equestrian traditions of Borgu. The reoccupation of Oyo-Ile under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abipa">Alaafin Abipa</a> around 1610 opened the empire’s genuine expansionary phase, as successive rulers extended Oyo’s tributary network westward and southward towards the Atlantic coast. Repeated invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> from 1726 onwards, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#agaja">King Agaja</a>’s conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#allada">Allada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ouidah">Ouidah</a> had upset the coastal trading order, forced Dahomey to pay tribute under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ojigi">Alaafin Ojigi</a>, and a treaty of 1748 confirmed it as a tribute-paying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a>. This was the apogee of Oyo power, when its tributary network extended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-river">Niger</a> in the east across the Aja lands to the west, encompassing Dahomey, Allada and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ouidah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ouidah.jpg" alt="Ouidah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ouidah">Ouidah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Door_of_no_return.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco Anzola, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Oyo’s economic foundations rested on its commanding position on the trade routes linking the Atlantic coast with the interior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#savannah">savannah</a> and, beyond it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hausa">Hausa states</a> and trans-Saharan networks, which made it an indispensable intermediary in the exchange of European manufactures, Atlantic imports and the agricultural and pastoral products of the interior. The empire derived substantial revenue from tribute extracted from subordinate states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>’s annual tribute including cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cowrie-shell">cowrie</a> shells and enslaved persons, and from tolls levied on the long-distance caravans that crossed its territories. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> became increasingly central to Oyo’s fiscal and military economy from the late seventeenth century onwards: captives taken in war and raids were sold to European merchants at coastal ports, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ouidah">Ouidah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a> and Badagry, in exchange for firearms, textiles and luxury goods that reinforced the prestige and military capacity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alaafin">Alaafin</a> and the Oyo nobility. Agriculture across the savannah heartland produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millet</a> and yams, sustaining a dense population in walled towns and rural settlements governed by subordinate chiefs, while Oyo weavers and dyers produced cloth for regional markets. The cowrie shell served as the standard currency, facilitating market exchange across considerable distances. The empire’s entanglement with the Atlantic slave trade, though profitable during the expansionary period, created dependencies and regional depopulation that destabilised its tributary periphery and fed the instability of the late eighteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#niger-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niger-river.jpg" alt="Niger River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#niger-river">Niger River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niger,_Niamey,_Pont_Kennedy_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NigerTZai, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Oyo’s military supremacy in the region rested above all on its cavalry, the most powerful horse-based force between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-river">Niger</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volta-river">Volta</a>, whose effectiveness depended on the open <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#savannah">savannah</a> of the empire’s core and on the steady importation of horses from the north, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tsetse-fly">tsetse-fly</a> zones of the forest made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#horse-breeding">horse-breeding</a> impossible further south. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eso">Eso</a>, an elite body of seventy war chiefs serving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alaafin">Alaafin</a>, formed the organisational nucleus around which larger campaign forces were assembled, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#are-ona-kakanfo">Are-Ona-Kakanfo</a>, the empire’s supreme field commander, was an appointed officer who, according to Oyo convention, was expected to win his campaigns or die in the attempt. Oyo campaigns were characterised by rapid, deep raids into enemy territory designed to seize captives, destroy economic infrastructure and demonstrate the futility of resistance, rather than the systematic occupation pursued by infantry-based empires. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> campaigns of the 1720s and 1730s showed both the reach and the limits of this approach: Oyo cavalry repeatedly devastated Dahomey but could not permanently hold its wooded southern territories and settled for tribute rather than annexation. The empire’s critical vulnerability was its inability to project cavalry power into the forest zones to the south, which meant that coastal politics were always managed through subordinate intermediaries rather than direct control, an indirect relationship that proved fragile when Dahomey under King Ghezo ceased paying tribute in the 1820s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#oyo-ile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oyo-ile.jpg" alt="Oyo-Ile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#oyo-ile">Oyo-Ile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OyoIle_plan_bw.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">http://csweb.bournemouth.ac.uk/africanlegacy/old_oyo.htm, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Oyo’s constitutional order was among the most sophisticated in precolonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-africa">West Africa</a>, a carefully balanced system of checks between royal authority and aristocratic oversight. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alaafin">Alaafin</a>, a sacred king whose person embodied divine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yoruba">Yoruba</a> kingship and who ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afin">Afin</a>, the royal palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oyo-ile">Oyo-Ile</a>, held supreme authority in theory but was constrained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oyo-mesi">Oyo Mesi</a>, a council of seven high chiefs headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basorun">Basorun</a>. This council held the recognised power to reject an Alaafin judged to be ruling against the interests of the state, signalled by the presentation of an empty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#calabash">calabash</a> or parrots’ eggs, after which the king was expected to take his own life. Intended to prevent tyranny, the mechanism proved dangerously open to abuse: during the ascendancy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basorun-gaa">Basorun Gaa</a>, from about 1754 to 1774, the Basorun is said to have driven several successive Alaafins to their deaths and effectively ruled the empire himself, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abiodun">Alaafin Abiodun</a> built a coalition, with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#are-ona-kakanfo">Are-Ona-Kakanfo</a> Oyabi, that overthrew and executed him around 1774. Provincial government operated through subordinate rulers supervised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ajele">Ajele</a>, royal agents stationed in tributary towns, whose exactions generated chronic resentment in the periphery. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ogboni-society">Ogboni society</a>, a religious and judicial association representing the earth cult and the free citizens, provided an additional counterweight, mediating disputes and maintaining social cohesion independently of the palace.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ibadan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibadan.jpg" alt="Ibadan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ibadan">Ibadan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_the_National_Museum_of_Unity,_Aleshinloye,_Ibadan_2024-06-02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ExploreWithTunde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Oyo’s most far-reaching global impact operated through its central role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, which made it a key node in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#triangular-trade">triangular commerce</a> connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-africa">West Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> during the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. Oyo-controlled trade networks are estimated to have contributed several hundred thousand captives to the Atlantic trade, and the wars that followed the empire’s collapse sent still larger numbers of Yoruba-speaking captives to the Americas in the early nineteenth century, above all to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a>, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bahia">Bahia</a>, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuba">Cuba</a>. Their religious practices became foundational to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#candomble">Candomblé</a> in Brazil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#santeria">Santería</a> (Lucumí) in Cuba, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yoruba">Yoruba</a>, or Nago, elements also entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou</a> alongside its predominantly Fon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> roots. Regionally, Oyo’s sustained pressure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> shaped the political development of the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a> coast, while the empire’s collapse produced massive population displacements that transformed the demographic and political geography of what is now south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Refugees and warriors from the Oyo heartland founded or enlarged new centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibadan">Ibadan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abeokuta">Abeokuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ijaye">Ijaye</a>, which became the leading Yoruba powers of the nineteenth century. Oyo’s place in Yoruba cultural memory remains profound, its constitutional ideas and traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> informing Yoruba political thought and identity long after the empire itself ceased to exist.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ilorin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilorin.jpg" alt="Ilorin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ilorin">Ilorin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilorin_palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eukoha, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Oyo’s collapse resulted from the combined effects of constitutional dysfunction, military overextension, provincial resentment and external religious and military pressure, which overwhelmed the empire within a remarkably short period between about 1790 and 1836. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basorun-gaa-crisis">Basorun Gaa crisis</a> had damaged the legitimacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oyo-mesi">Oyo Mesi</a> and left lasting mistrust between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alaafin">Alaafin</a> and the military aristocracy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abiodun">Alaafin Abiodun</a>’s long reign (c. 1770–1789), though stabilising, favoured Atlantic commercial revenues over military strength, allowing the army to deteriorate as external threats grew. Around 1796, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#awole">Alaafin Awole</a> was rejected and forced to take his own life after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#are-ona-kakanfo">Are-Ona-Kakanfo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afonja">Afonja</a> and other chiefs mutinied against his order to attack Iwere, a town under the Alaafin’s own sacred protection, shattering the compact between king and military elite. Around 1817 Afonja, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilorin">Ilorin</a>, rebelled openly and enlisted the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fula">Fulani</a> Muslim cleric Alimi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hausa">Hausa</a> and Fulani followers inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jihad">jihad</a>, introducing a formidable new enemy into the heart of the empire. Afonja was himself killed by his allies in the early 1820s, and Ilorin became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emirate">emirate</a> of the Sokoto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">Caliphate</a> whose reformist Islamic ideology rejected Oyo’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> and whose persistent military pressure, combined with internal fragmentation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>’s secession, made the empire’s position untenable. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oyo-ile">Oyo-Ile</a>, abandoned around 1835–1836, Alaafin Atiba established a reduced successor state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-oyo">New Oyo</a> further south, which signed a treaty with the British in 1888 and was later absorbed into colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nigeria">Nigeria</a>, where the Alaafin’s office survived as a traditional kingship.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Oyo Empire’s historical significance endures most visibly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#african-diaspora">African diaspora</a> it helped to create, a dispersal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yoruba">Yoruba</a> language, religion and cultural practice across the Atlantic world that produced living traditions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuba">Cuba</a>, ensuring that an empire which profited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and was ultimately overwhelmed by the wars that fed it achieved a form of cultural immortality in the societies built by its victims.</em></p>

